  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "cil_gen_validatetrans",
    "file": "selinux/libsepol/cil/src/cil_build_ast.c",
    "description": "`cil_gen_validatetrans` stores `validtrans->str_expr` using `cil_gen_constraint_expr(..., flavor, ...)` where `flavor` is `CIL_VALIDATETRANS`. This builds list items whose element flavor can be `CIL_VALIDATETRANS` instead of constraint-expression item flavors. During teardown, `cil_destroy_validatetrans` calls `cil_list_destroy(&validtrans->str_expr, CIL_TRUE)`, and `cil_list_item_destroy` then calls `cil_destroy_data(&item->data, item->flavor)`. Because item->flavor is wrong, `cil_destroy_data` dispatches to `cil_destroy_validatetrans` on non-validatetrans data, leading to invalid recursive destruction and a bad write at `cil.c:1016` (`*data = NULL`). Crash manifests in `cil_destroy_data`, but root cause is wrong flavor propagation in `cil_gen_validatetrans`.",
    "conditions": "Trigger with a malformed `validatetrans` expression that causes `cil_gen_constraint_expr` to produce expression-list items and then fail/cleanup path (or later DB destroy) to free them. PoC `(validatetrans f(eq t3(P)))` reaches this path: parser builds `validtrans->str_expr` with incorrect item flavor derived from parent `validatetrans`, then `cil_db_destroy` -> tree destroy -> `cil_destroy_validatetrans` -> `cil_list_destroy` -> `cil_destroy_data` misinterprets list item data as `struct cil_validatetrans*`, causing SEGV."
  },
